\section{Synthesis \& Empirical Findings}
To evaluate how fiscal burdens and rewards distribute across demographic cohorts over time, we construct a population-normalized, inflation-adjusted Generational Accounting panel in constant 2024 Canadian dollars (\$\\text\{2024 CAD\}\$) spanning 1985 to 2025. Integrating Statistics Canada’s Distributions of Household Economic Accounts (DHEA) and Social Transfers in Kind (STiK) with CIHI age-specific expenditure curves, we define the real per-capita Net Fiscal Incidence (\$\\text\{NFI\}\_\{g,t\}\$) for generation \$g\$ in period \$t\$ as total direct and indirect taxes paid minus cash transfers and in-kind public services received, normalized by the cohort's population weight \$P\_\{g,t\} / P\_t\$:
\$\$\\text\{NFI\}\_\{g,t\} = \\frac\{1\}\{P\_\{g,t\} \\cdot \\pi\_t\} \\left[ \\sum\_\{k \\in \\mathcal\{K\}\} \\tau\_\{k,g,t\} B\_\{k,g,t\} - \\left( \\text\{Cash\}\_\{g,t\} + \\text\{STiK\}\^\{\\text\{Health\}\}\_\{g,t\} + \\text\{STiK\}\^\{\\text\{Educ\}\}\_\{g,t\} \\right) \\right], \\quad \\Gamma\_\{g,t\} = \\frac\{\\text\{Rewards\}\_\{g,t\}\}\{\\text\{Taxes\}\_\{g,t\}\}\$\$
where \$\\pi\_t = \\text\{CPI\}\_t / \\text\{CPI\}\_\{2024\}\$ is the price deflator, \$\\tau\_\{k,g,t\}\$ is the effective tax rate on tax base \$B\_\{k,g,t\}\$ across instrument set \$\\mathcal\{K\} = \\\{\\text\{income, sales, property, fuel/carbon, alcohol, vape\}\\\}\$, and \$\\Gamma\_\{g,t\}\$ is the Fiscal Reward-to-Tax Ratio. Empirically, prime-working-age Generations X (ages 45–60) and Millennials (ages 29–44) carry the heaviest gross real per-capita tax

